round six of more things you didn't know  about breath of the wild  this time we're gonna dip our toes again  into more secrets about the game's  development and game files  so if you continue to enjoy this series  support us by liking this video  it helps a bunch and here we go  [Music]  it's possible to make a pet guardian  that keeps returning to you  in a previous video i showed how to  disable a guardian using urbosa's fury  and stasis  but a side effect of this is if they get  pushed or launched away  they will always return to the point  that got disabled which means you can  launch them using cryonis  and have them walk back to you like a  dog and if you wanted to launch over  with them  and go for a quick ride  this is different from the old guardian  riding technique because this is  actually a friendly guardian  who's a good boy most skyward sword fans  saw this hard to misreference at the  spring of power when breath of the wild  first released but there's another  skyward sword reference that is not so  obvious  the wall designs on skyloft's walls are  seen at lenairu promenade  all old and worn down while it's  completely impractical to do the fact  that you can  launch an item stasis the next object it  hits  transfer that momentum then repeat onto  the next object  just shows how cool breath of the wild's  engine really is  and a testament to how well it works  there are two enemies in the game that  don't break your weapon when you throw a  weapon at them  malicize can be destroyed when you throw  a weapon at them and bees are  immediately dispersed when you do the  same  it's even considered a critical hit  on the same note there's also a  technique called unbroken throw that  allows you to throw against enemies  without breaking your weapon  by first striking an object before it  touches the enemy the weapon doesn't  break  the easiest way to do this is with the  master cycle now you can throw freely  without worrying about losing your  weapon  you can headshot stall enemies even when  their bones are still a pile in the  ground  did you know that all explosive barrels  are not created equal  where some barrels did massive damage  while others only stung a bit  that's because there's actually three  different damage values for explosive  barrels  including the additional fire damage  weaker barrels do 10 damage  middle barrels do 46 damage and strong  barrels do a whopping 86 damage  unfortunately there's no way to tell  them apart visually so here's a map of  the different damaged barrels  specific style coblins have some really  strange properties when they die  instead of falling apart they slowly  float up to heaven  there is one deciduous tree aka non-pine  tree that exists in snowy areas  this single tree south of north lomay  labyrinth it even grows  out of solid bedrock weird while swift  carrots may seem plentiful and easy to  get because of kakariko village  this is actually one of the rarest wild  ingredients in the game with only a  handful of them on satori mountain  and a singular carrot someone dropped  behind the horses at the highland stable  did you know that moblins can throw the  barrels you can hide in  there are many places throughout the  game that block horses from entering  called horse barriers most of these have  a roof though so if you can drop down on  them from above with your horse  you can walk around on thin air  normally rocks have a random chance to  drop loot under them  but several rocks in hyrule are hard  coated to give you specific drop table  rewards every time  such as ruby only rocks or only rocks  and special ones such as this rock as a  tory mountain which always gives you a  diamond  and this rock in front of the old man  which always gives you a high tail  lizard which was likely designed to  teach and reward  new players to pick up rocks which  normally would be a low chance at random  rewards  there's a mysterious asset named air  wall for e3  still in the game's file originally  designed to keep players out from  accessing certain areas in the breath of  the wild e3 2016 demo  strange thing is that this same asset is  still used in the game but is barely  ever seen  with almost all of them with collisions  set to false so link simply walks  through this invisible barrier but there  are some  e3 air walls that still work such as the  holes you shoot through as sheem the  ghost shrine  and the wall surrounding the great  plateau tower to prevent you from  falling too far away from the tower  bottom  did you know that the wolf link amiibo  helps you search for items you pinged on  your radar  when nearby a searched item it will head  towards it and wait for you to pick it  up  on the same note if you search for a  rusty broadsword here at the gerudo  canyon pass  it says it's nearby but nothing is found  it seems like it would pair well with a  single rusty shield on top of this rock  and it does if you decide to clip into  the rock below the rusty sword is hidden  inside  sitting on top of the stone below breath  of the wild has a certain type of rocky  terrain that you've definitely seen  before as a barrier or a cliffside but  what you might not know is what its  official name is  the official asset name by nintendo for  these rocks are  cliff cheese wall cliff cheese stone and  cliff cheese rock  now i'm hungry there is one apple near  the horwell bridge that's buried  underground  barely noticeable but did you know that  round bombs are thrown farther than  square bombs  the fastest way to gain a horse's trust  is by spurring quickly  breaking then soothing then accelerating  again  literally speed running a horse's  friendship  if you headshot this moblin in hyrule  castle while it's on the stairs  he'll uh go for a little ride  i remember doing that when i was a kid  there is some secret dialogue you will  never see  unless you went out of your way to  reload certain conversations  such as with barda during the search for  barda quest where the conversation will  be completely different if you have  gerudo armor  on or off did you know you could protect  yourself against thorns with deruk's  protection without actually cracking the  shield  this might seem straightforward for some  players but most players still don't  know that you can simply switch runes  through the rune explanation menu  [Music]  did you know that the hp scanning system  used to be an ability that the sheikah  slate would always have  you used to be able to scan enemies from  a distance and display their hp  just like the champion's tunic or  ancient armor in hindsight i'm glad  they went with the armor idea because  running around and scanning enemies  doesn't really sound fun  when disarmed style zoflows like to  attack just like any other  zolfos empty-handed by tail whips or  tongue attacks  except it has no tongue or guts so when  it tries to  nothing happens it's trying its best  this is what breath of the wild's map  looked like back in 2014  and this is what it looks like now it  might not be so obvious  in normal gameplay because once enemies  see you they don't really care about  anything else and simply attack you  but most enemies such as moblins lost  and bokoblins are actually terrified of  guardians  there are a ton of things in the game  that can be parried but did you know  that you can parry a fire chuchu jelly  while ice and shock jellies aren't  blockable fire jelly's explosions are  actually considered a strike  and can be parried the guardian  skywatcher's weak spot is a sheika  symbol right on its top  the interesting thing though is that the  guardian turrets strewn around hyrule  castle are all just  upside down skywatchers with these weak  spots protected since they're right side  up  but if you clip underground you can just  one shot them from underneath by  exploiting the same weakness  and that's a wrap hopefully you learned  something new again today to show off to  your friends  if you like this series and more  breakdowns check out our breath of the  wild expert series playlist  and of course for everything else gaming  stay tuned right here on gamespot  [Music]  you
 

